<h2 id="overview-heading">Understanding Conveyor Components</h2>
<h3>Frame &amp; Structure</h3>
<p>The frame carries the conveying surface, the drive, the product load, and every mounted accessory. Carbon steel is the default for dry environments, stainless steel for washdown and food contact, and aluminum extrusion for light-duty and reconfigurable lines. Frame choice also sets the material for bearings, fasteners, and guide rail brackets. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/components/">frame, drive, and pulley design guide</a> covers how to specify the frame alongside the drive and take-up.</p>
<h3>Product Handling Components</h3>
<p><a href="/components/guide-rails/">Conveyor guide rails</a>, also called side rails or side guides, keep products positioned on the conveyor and channel them into lanes for downstream operations. Side guides prevent edge falloff on belt conveyors. Lane dividers create parallel product streams on wide conveyors. Adjustable guide systems allow quick changeover between product sizes without tools. These components are typically made from UHMW polyethylene (low friction, food-grade grades available), stainless steel (durability, washdown), or aluminum with UHMW contact strips.</p>
<h3>Cleaning &amp; Sanitation</h3>
<p><a href="/components/cleaning-systems/">Conveyor cleaning systems</a> maintain sanitary conditions on <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food and beverage lines</a> and remove carryback on bulk material conveyors. Belt cleaners (scrapers, brushes) mount at the head pulley to remove residue. Washdown systems use water, foam, or sanitizer to clean conveyor surfaces during changeovers or sanitation breaks. <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">Pharmaceutical</a> and cleanroom applications require specialized cleaning protocols and equipment.</p>
<h3>Conveying Surface Components</h3>
<p>The conveying surface is the primary wear component on any conveyor. <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">Conveyor belts</a> come in many materials and constructions: PVC/PU fabric belts for general packaging, modular plastic belts for food and wet environments, wire mesh for high-temperature applications, and timing belts for precision positioning. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">Roller conveyors</a> use gravity rollers, powered rollers, or <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR motorized rollers</a> as the conveying surface.</p>
<h3>Engineering Plastics: POM, UHMW, Nylon</h3>
<p>Many wear and contact components are molded or machined from engineering plastics rather than metal.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Material</th>
<th>Typical conveyor components</th>
<th>Why it is chosen</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>POM (polyoxymethylene, acetal)</td>
<td>Modular plastic belt modules, tabletop chain, sprockets, rollers, bushings</td>
<td>Stiff, low friction, dimensionally stable. <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-2400/tight-turning-radius" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intralox</a> rates its acetal Series 2400 belt for -50 to 200°F.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>UHMW polyethylene</td>
<td>Guide rails, wear strips, chain guides, lane dividers</td>
<td>Low friction and high abrasion resistance, easy to machine</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Nylon (polyamide)</td>
<td>Hinge rods, rollers, gears, bushings</td>
<td>Tough and wear resistant, but absorbs moisture, which affects dimensions in wet areas</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Polypropylene</td>
<td>Modular belt modules for wet and chemical exposure</td>
<td>Chemical resistance, light weight</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For food contact, POM copolymer is listed in <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/177.2470" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 177.2470</a> for articles intended for repeated food-contact use, under stated extractive limits and at temperatures not exceeding 250°F. Ask the component supplier for a food-contact compliance letter naming the regulation for the exact grade. Check the rated temperature range and cleaning chemistry before choosing POM, nylon, or polypropylene for a given part.</p>
<h3>Drive Components</h3>
<p><a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">Motors and drives</a> provide the power that moves the conveyor. AC gearmotors are the standard drive for most packaging conveyors. Variable frequency drives (VFDs) allow speed adjustment and soft starting. Servo drives enable precise positioning for <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing applications</a>. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR systems</a> use 24VDC motors built into the conveyor rollers themselves.</p>
<h3>Overhead Conveyor Components</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/overhead/">Overhead conveyors</a> use a different component set from floor-mounted belt and roller conveyors. The core parts are the track (I-beam or enclosed track), the chain, trolleys that ride on or in the track, carriers or hangers that hold the product, a drive unit, and a take-up that keeps chain tension as the chain wears. Power-and-free systems add a separate free track plus stops and switches so individual carriers can accumulate or change paths. Load per trolley, track layout, and the environment (paint booths, ovens, washers) set the specification.</p>
<h3>Wear Parts &amp; Replacement</h3>
<p><a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">Conveyor parts</a> that require regular replacement include belts, rollers, bearings, wear strips, O-rings (on line-shaft conveyors), and sensors. Understanding replacement intervals, OEM versus aftermarket sourcing, and spare parts inventory strategy reduces unplanned downtime and maintenance cost. The parts guide covers identification, sourcing, and stocking recommendations for all major wear components.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Component Selection Criteria</h2>
<p>Selecting conveyor components requires matching specifications to your application requirements. Key factors include:</p>
<p><strong>1. Material compatibility.</strong> Food and pharmaceutical applications require food-contact compliant materials (food-grade UHMW, POM, and other plastics, stainless steel). Wet or washdown environments need corrosion-resistant materials. High-temperature applications require heat-resistant materials and lubricants.</p>
<p><strong>2. Product characteristics.</strong> Product weight determines bearing and roller capacity. Product fragility affects guide rail padding and contact pressure. Product dimensions determine guide rail spacing and belt width. Product surface (smooth, rough, oily) affects belt grip and cleaning requirements.</p>
<p><strong>3. Environment.</strong> Ambient temperature affects lubricant selection and plastic material choice. Humidity and washdown frequency affect corrosion resistance requirements. Dust and debris levels affect sealing and cleaning frequency.</p>
<p><strong>4. Speed and throughput.</strong> Higher speeds require more robust bearings, better belt tracking, and more frequent maintenance. High throughput applications may justify premium components with longer service life.</p>
<p><strong>5. Maintenance capability.</strong> Components that require frequent adjustment or replacement should be easy to access. Quick-change guide rails reduce changeover time. Tool-free belt tensioning speeds maintenance. Standard components from multiple suppliers ensure availability.</p>
<h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
<ul class="sources">
<li><a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.219" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">29 CFR 1910.219 Mechanical power-transmission apparatus</a>, OSHA. Guarding of drives, pulleys, and belts.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">B20.1 Safety Standard for Conveyors and Related Equipment</a>, ASME. Conveyor safety design, installation, and operation.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/177.2470" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 177.2470 Polyoxymethylene copolymer</a>, Code of Federal Regulations (via Cornell LII). Food-contact use of POM copolymer, 250°F limit.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-2400/tight-turning-radius" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Series 2400 Tight Turning Radius</a>, Intralox. Acetal and polypropylene belt materials, acetal temperature range.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What are the main components of a conveyor system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A conveyor system is built from the frame and support structure, the conveying surface (<a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">belt</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">rollers</a>, or chain), the drive (<a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">motor, gearbox</a>, and drive pulley or sprocket), tensioning parts (tail pulley, take-up, and bearings), product handling accessories (<a href="/components/guide-rails/">guide rails</a>, lane dividers, <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfers</a>), controls and sensors, and guarding. Each category is specified for the product, load, speed, and environment, and choices in one category constrain the others.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What are conveyor guide rails used for?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><a href="/components/guide-rails/">Conveyor guide rails</a>, also called side rails or side guides, keep products centered on the conveyor, prevent products from falling off the edges, create lanes for multi-product flow, and orient products for downstream operations like labeling or <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packing</a>. They are typically made from UHMW polyethylene, stainless steel, or aluminum with UHMW contact surfaces. Adjustable guide rails allow quick changeover between product sizes.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What are the components of an overhead conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/overhead/">Overhead conveyors</a> use a different component set from floor conveyors. The main parts are the track (I-beam or enclosed track), the chain, trolleys that ride in or on the track, carriers or hangers that hold the product, a drive unit, and a take-up that keeps chain tension. Power-and-free systems add a separate free track plus stops and switches so individual carriers can accumulate or change paths.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do I choose the right conveyor belt cleaner?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt cleaner selection depends on the belt type, product residue characteristics, and sanitation requirements. Primary cleaners mount at the head pulley to scrape bulk material. Secondary cleaners mount behind the primary for finer cleaning. For <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food applications</a>, FDA-compliant blade materials and washdown-rated designs are required. For heavy carryback, consider urethane or tungsten carbide blades. For light dust, brush cleaners may suffice. See the <a href="/components/cleaning-systems/">cleaning systems guide</a> for detailed selection criteria.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What conveyor components need regular replacement?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The components replaced most often are the ones in sliding or rolling contact: belts, rollers and bearings, wear strips and UHMW guide rail surfaces, O-rings on line-shaft conveyors, and sensors damaged in service. <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">Motors and gearboxes</a> usually last longer. Actual intervals depend on load, speed, duty cycle, cleaning chemistry, and maintenance, so use the manufacturer's recommendations and your own failure history to set replacement schedules. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">conveyor parts guide</a> covers spares planning.</p>

</div>
</div>
